Subject: GTK on Phoenix: the web process renders one frame ahead of the GTK UI (opt-in)

WebKitGTK 3's AcceleratedBackingStore sends FrameDone for a web-process frame from paint(), after
GDK's frame clock has drawn it, and the web process's ThreadedCompositor composites the next frame
only after FrameDone (State::InProgress until frameComplete()). So exactly one frame is in flight
and the two processes run in series: on the Raspberry Pi 4 (B10 §11, the paint watch of 0105)
~15 ms of GTK work per frame plus ~20 ms of web-process rendering, 33-41 ms per frame, a ~30 fps
cap: a 60 fps video painted 47 % of its frames, MotionMark-quick scored 2.60 (WPE: 74.99).

With WEBKIT_PHOENIX_FRAME_AHEAD=1 (webkit-browser --frame-ahead) FrameDone goes when a frame is
received, so the web process renders frame N+1 while GTK paints frame N; the rate becomes that of
the slower side instead of the sum of both (1 / max(15, 20 ms) on the Pi), latency grows by up to
one frame. As WPE's patch 0019 does for its compositor, the web process stays at most one frame
ahead of what GTK draws:

  - at most one undrawn frame: a frame that arrives before GTK drew the previous one (the web
    process was faster than GTK's paint) replaces it; the replaced frame's buffer goes back to the
    web process (ReleaseBuffer) undrawn, and the paint watch counts it as superseded. The new
    frame's FrameDone then waits until it is current (paint()), so the web process does not render
    frames that are never shown; the next frame is again early.
  - exactly one FrameDone per Frame (counted: frame_done, of which done_early when received).
  - buffers: the buffer GTK draws (the committed one) is released only by swapBuffersIfNeeded(),
    as upstream, once the next frame replaced it, so the web process never renders into a buffer
    GTK is sampling; its swap chain (AcceleratedSurface::SwapChain, at most 4 targets, 2 made at
    first) then holds the committed, the pending and the rendering target: 3.
  - the frame clock: frame() only queues a redraw; GDK still paints at most once per cycle.
  - with a rendering fence still pending (never on Phoenix: patch 0016 keeps the fences in the web
    process) FrameDone goes after paint() as before, since a second frame would find the fence
    monitor busy.

Off by default, so one image can A/B both pacings in one boot. The UI process says which one a
view uses, once per backing store (the line of WPE's 0019):

  WPEB-WEBKIT frame-pacing pid=<p> ahead=0|1

